{"answer":"VERDICT: conceded in part, overruled in consequence. The 'equivalently' clause, read as a biconditional, is false; I verified the 16-vertex counterexample and accept it. It is corrected to a one-way implication. However, the false direction is exactly the one the derivation never uses, every load-bearing step of the main argument survives verbatim, and the counterexample, correctly booked, only enlarges the class of mixed absorbing traps and therefore reinforces the thesis.\n\n1) CONCESSION AND CORRECTED STATEMENT. Accepted: there is a 16-vertex cubic graph carrying a stable mixed coloring in which some monochromatic component's degree-3 vertices admit no perfect matching; for that coloring no perfect matching M' with monochromatic 2-factor complement exists, so stable does not imply perfect-matching-extension. The correct statement is one-way: if M' is a perfect matching of a 3-regular graph G and each cycle of the 2-factor G - M' is colored monochromatically, with at least two cycles in different colors, the resulting coloring is a stable mixed configuration. Two subsidiary points: (a) the min-degree clause of the original statement added no restriction, since every stable coloring already gives each vertex at least two monochromatic edges, so the clause was automatically satisfied and striking it changes nothing; (b) the genuinely extra condition the converse would need, a perfect matching on the degree-3 vertices within each monochromatic component, is exactly what the counterexample refutes. The honest repair is to drop the converse, not to patch it.\n\n2) THE FALSE DIRECTION IS INERT. The lemma is used only in the absorber-counting step and only constructively: from a perfect matching, exhibit a stable mixed absorbing configuration. That direction is valid and, in 3-regular graphs, nearly automatic: deleting a perfect matching leaves a spanning 2-factor by degree count alone; each vertex then keeps its two cycle-neighbors in its own color, which is precisely the stability criterion; two differently colored cycles make the configuration mixed. The converse, that every stable mixed coloring arises from such a decomposition, appears nowhere: no enumeration, no upper bound, and no probability estimate assumes the absorbing configurations are exhausted by the perfect-matching family.\n\n3) THE exp(Θ(n)) PREVALENCE CLAIM IS INVARIANT UNDER EITHER READING OF 'obstruction'. Narrow reading (only perfect-matching-generated configurations): the lower bound is inherited from the perfect-matching subfamily and rests on the undisputed premise that random 3-regular graphs have exp(Θ(n)) perfect matchings, each generating mixed absorbing configurations via the valid direction of clause 2; the upper bound is trivial, since there are at most 2^n = exp(O(n)) colorings in total. Broad reading (all stable mixed configurations, the counterexample type included): still at most 2^n, and at least the narrow family. Both readings give exp(Θ(n)); no downstream step distinguishes them, because the main argument consumes only 'at least exponentially many mixed absorbers, at most exponentially many configurations'.\n\n4) THE COUNTEREXAMPLE REINFORCES THE THESIS. The thesis is that mixed absorbing traps are abundant relative to the at most two consensus states, so the dynamics on the random cubic graph overwhelmingly terminates mixed. That argument needs abundance of traps, not an exact classification of them. The counterexample strictly enlarges the trap class, from the perfect-matching family to all stable mixed colorings, so the abundance asymmetry can only grow. On the ledger the counterexample is a witness for the thesis, not against it.\n\n5) THE MAIN CLAIM'S DEPENDENCIES ARE UNTOUCHED. (i) The monotone decrease of the potential along the dynamics is not disputed and is unimplicated: the counterexample concerns a static coloring, not a trajectory. (ii) The operative absorbing-state characterization, that a coloring is absorbing iff its bichromatic edges form a matching, equivalently every vertex has at least two of its three neighbors in its own color, is exact and automatic in 3-regular graphs; the flawed 'equivalently' was an auxiliary strengthening of this, not this characterization itself. (iii) The abundance asymmetry is invariant, by clause 3. (iv) Basin sizes: the one genuine countercondition, that the mixed absorbers' basins are not collectively exponentially negligible, was already explicitly self-flagged in the original argument as the standing vulnerability. The critic's case does not raise it, and a static classification counterexample has no bearing on it; if anything, additional absorber families enlarge the collective absorbing set.\n\n6) WHAT REMAINS OPEN, RECORDED HONESTLY. Whether non-extendable stable colorings occur whp in the random cubic model is established neither way: the 16-vertex example proves existence in some cubic graph, not prevalence in the ensemble. Recorded as open; the main argument depends on neither answer.\n\nNET LEDGER: one clause conceded and corrected (the biconditional becomes a one-way implication; the vacuous min-degree clause is struck; the extra perfect-matching condition is acknowledged to fail); zero clauses of the main argument overturned; the counterexample re-booked as supporting evidence; the real vulnerability remains the self-flagged basin-size condition, which this case does not touch."}